{
  "situation": "Fraud model v2 went live for SIM-replacement requests at 08:00 and refused SIM replacements are up about tenfold, which the fraud dashboard labels a 'fraud wave'; after the review queue was enabled, OpCo's twin reports legit_pass_rate 1.0 and locked_out_banking 0, so no legitimate customer appears locked out of banking. We do not know whether a PDPL Article 21(1) impact evaluation was carried out before model v2 went live, whether the tenfold spike is independently confirmed as fraud rather than model error, or how the twin metrics used to validate the model are governed and validated.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Fraud model v2 went live for SIM-replacement requests at 08:00 and refused SIM replacements rose about tenfold (S3-I1); the review queue is enabled and twin metrics now show legit_pass_rate 1.0 and locked_out_banking 0 (S3-FB01).",
        "rule": "UAE PDPL Article (1) defines Personal Data Security as technical and organisational measures, procedures and processes that maintain the protection of privacy, confidentiality, integrity and availability of Personal Data.",
        "inference": "The SIM-replacement refusals, model v2 scoring decisions and review-queue dispositions are the evidence base for both the fraud investigation and any later data-protection challenge, so they must be preserved before routine log rotation removes them."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0002",
          "quote": "A set of technical and organizational measures, procedures and processes specified in accordance with provisions of this Decree by Law which maintain the protection of privacy, confidentiality, integrity and availability of Personal Data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "OpCo must be able to show an Article 21(1) impact evaluation on the protection of Personal Data for the automated processing introduced by fraud model v2, which began refusing SIM-replacement requests at 08:00.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Model v2 is an automated system that refuses SIM-replacement requests and went live at 08:00 (S3-I1); automated processing is processing carried out by an electronic program or system operating automatically, with no or limited human intervention.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 21(1) requires the Controller, before carrying out the processing, to evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data when using modern technologies that pose a high risk to privacy and confidentiality.",
        "inference": "SIM replacement is the authentication root for banking and other services, so automated refusal decisions on it are high-risk processing; the Article 21(1) evaluation for model v2 is owed and must exist on record, regardless of how the refusal volume is later classified."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data"
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0002",
          "quote": "Processing which is carried out using an electronic program or system which operates in an automated and automatic manner either completely independently without any human intervention or partially with limited human supervision and intervention"
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "There is no rule, gate or named owner requiring a documented impact assessment and a risk re-review before an automated fraud model is switched on or changed for SIM-replacement decisions, so model v2 went live at 08:00 and its risk was assessed only after a tenfold rise in refusals.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "OpCo switched on fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement decisions at 08:00 (S3-I1) and reports its outcome only through twin metrics (S3-FB01); the inbox shows no prior impact evaluation, no re-review trigger and no owner for validating the model's refusals.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 21(1) requires an impact evaluation before high-risk processing, but the UAE text shown imposes no documentation gate, no change-triggered re-review and no accountable owner for automated decision models.",
        "inference": "Without an internal rule that makes the Article 21(1) evaluation a pre-deployment condition and re-triggers it when the model's risk changes, a model can be deployed first and questioned only after the refusal rate moves; the EU text shows the re-review trigger that is missing here."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "1. Taking into account the nature, scope and purposes of data processing, the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk to the privacy and confidentiality of the Data Subject's Personal Data."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040",
          "quote": "Such types of processing operations may be those which in, particular, involve using new technologies, or are of a new kind and where no data protection impact assessment has been carried out before by the controller"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "statement": "Fraud model v2 went live for SIM-replacement requests at 08:00; refused SIM replacements are up about tenfold and are labelled a 'fraud wave'. OpCo has enabled human review of borderline automated refusals, and its twin metrics now show legit_pass_rate 1.0 with locked_out_banking 0. OpCo does not yet hold independent confirmation that the spike is genuine fraud rather than model error, and the Article 21(1) impact evaluation position for model v2 is not yet evidenced."
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The excerpts do not establish whether the tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements is confirmed external fraud rather than model error, nor how the digital-twin metrics (legit_pass_rate 1.0, locked_out_banking 0) are produced, validated or governed; the only performance evidence available is OpCo's own twin output.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "ITU-T-Y3090#0030",
          "quote": "a single source of truth for the network twin network is formed, which provides accurate and complete information for various network applications"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "Fraud model v2, live for SIM-replacement requests from 08:00, produced a roughly tenfold jump in refusals — about 2,750 legitimate requests refused since 08:00, concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles — so we rolled back to v1, routed borderline refusals to human review and offered a non-SMS confirmation route to the bank-flagged cohort, which changed nothing in our telemetry, and there is still no human review path for a request already refused. We do not have a measured false-positive rate for v2 or v1, cannot yet separate genuine fraud from false positives, and have no sight of the banks', the Bureau's or the Central Bank's own evidence on the locked-out subscribers.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG has opened an examination of OpCo's automated SIM-replacement refusals and asked for the human-review record; the ~2,750 refusals, the v2 rollback and the review-queue change all need an auditable trail.",
        "rule": "PDPL requires a controller to maintain a special record of the personal data it processes and to submit that record to the Bureau when asked.",
        "inference": "Preserving logs and the human-review evidence now keeps that record complete and producible to REG and the Bureau."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0032",
          "quote": "Maintain a special record of Personal Data which is processed on behalf of the Controller, provided that such record includes the data of the Controller"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "REG",
        "content": "OpCo status on SIM-replacement refusals (11:00): fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 and was rolled back to v1; borderline automated refusals are routed to human review; a non-SMS confirmation route was offered to the bank-flagged cohort and produced no change in our telemetry. Analysis shows about 2,750 legitimate requests refused since 08:00, concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles, with known fraud still blocked and no human review path for an already-refused request. No false-positive rate for v2 or v1 is available yet; the human-review record is preserved and can be supplied on request."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB05", "S3-FB06", "S3-I3", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG asked to be sent any notification OpCo makes to TDRA plus measurement of the false-positive rate, and DPA has already flagged the refusal wave to TDRA.",
        "rule": "The record of processing must be submitted to the Bureau whenever it is requested, making the regulator the recipient for OpCo's incident facts.",
        "inference": "Notifying REG with the measured facts — rollback, review queue, refusal count, profile concentration, absence of a review path and absence of a false-positive rate — lets the sector and data-protection responses proceed without duplication or contradiction."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0032",
          "quote": "provided that the Processor submits such record to the Bureau whenever it is requested to do so."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "data_subjects",
        "content": "If our automated fraud checks refused your SIM-replacement request on or after 08:00, you may ask OpCo for the decision to be reviewed by a person and to exercise your data-protection rights. Fraud model v2 has been rolled back to v1 and borderline refusals are queued for human review; a non-SMS confirmation route is available to the bank-flagged cohort. Contact details are in your account and on the OpCo app."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-FB06"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Around 2,750 legitimate SIM-replacement requests were refused since 08:00 and there is no human review path for a request already refused; subscribers abroad are locked out of online banking.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 19 requires the controller to provide clear and appropriate ways for the data subject to contact it to request the rights set out in the Decree.",
        "inference": "Affected subscribers can only obtain human review or correction if OpCo tells them how and where to ask."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063",
          "quote": "The Controller shall provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights set forth in this Decree by Law."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "On the request of a data subject, OpCo must include the human element in reviewing the automated decisions that refused their SIM-replacement request.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The v2 refusals were automated decisions, at scale, and REG's examination together with the DPA alert concerns exactly those decisions.",
        "rule": "PDPL requires the controller to include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the data subject.",
        "inference": "OpCo must keep a review route open and staffed for the refusals already made, not only for future borderline cases."
      },
      "deadline_hours": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063",
          "quote": "The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "OpCo must give data subjects clear and appropriate means to contact it so they can request review of the refused SIM replacements and their other PDPL rights.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-I2"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Subscribers abroad are locked out of banking after refusals and have no obvious route to contest them.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 19 obliges the controller to provide clear and appropriate ways to contact it for the exercise of rights.",
        "inference": "A published, reachable contact route is required now, not only after the Bureau's examination concludes."
      },
      "deadline_hours": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063",
          "quote": "The Controller shall provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights set forth in this Decree by Law."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "When the Bureau requests it, OpCo must submit its record of the personal data processing behind the refusals, including the human-review record REG has asked for.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG03", "S3-I3"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG (the Bureau) has opened an examination and has requested the human-review record from NOC.",
        "rule": "PDPL requires the processor to submit its special record of processing to the Bureau whenever it is requested to do so.",
        "inference": "The refusal records, review-queue entries and model versions must be assembled and ready for submission."
      },
      "deadline_hours": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0032",
          "quote": "provided that the Processor submits such record to the Bureau whenever it is requested to do so."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "No rule requires that a SIM replacement already refused by automation be reviewable: the PDPL human-element duty is triggered only at the data subject's request and prescribes no route, no owner and no deadline, so a refused subscriber may simply be left without service.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Only borderline refusals go to human review; there is no human review path for a request already refused, and the refusals concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text gives a human-review right only when the data subject asks for it, without prescribing how the request is made or handled.",
        "inference": "A proactive review or appeal step for final automated refusals is a policy gap that this incident exposes."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063",
          "quote": "The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0017",
          "quote": "We emphasize, however, that our rules create an ongoing obligation that wireless providers ensure the authentication methods they use are secure."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0029",
          "quote": "we permit them to do so in instances when they determine these measures are necessary"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_owner",
      "statement": "No rule in the record assigns responsibility for a subscriber who is stranded because the SIM replacement was refused and the bank depends on SMS-delivered one-time passwords; the non-SMS route offered to the bank-flagged cohort produced no change, so neither OpCo nor the banks own the end-to-end outcome.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2", "S3-FB06", "S3-I3"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Banks report customers abroad cannot receive OTPs and are locked out, while the alternative non-SMS confirmation route for the bank-flagged cohort changed nothing in telemetry.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is the banking-sector move away from SMS and email OTPs, which crosses the boundary between the bank's authentication and OpCo's SIM-replacement confirmation.",
        "inference": "The handover between operator refusal and bank authentication failure has no owner and no rule."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0000",
          "quote": "UAE banks will stop sending OTPs via SMS and email for online transactions by July 25, 2025."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0001",
          "quote": "Customers travelling outside the UAE have complained of not receiving the SMS containing the OTP or receiving it after the valid time period."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0017",
          "quote": "authentication for SIM change requests, but we strongly encourage wireless providers to use this mechanism only when paired with other secure methods of authentication, i.e., as part of multi-factor authentication (MFA)."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0025",
          "quote": "we permit wireless providers to use existing methods of notification that are reasonably designed to reach the customer associated with the account"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "No deadline is stated for OpCo to report a mass automated refusal event to TDRA or to complete remediation for the subscribers already refused; the only reporting trigger found is a request from the Bureau.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Refusals have run since 08:00 and OpCo has supplied status only because REG asked; no deadline is quoted anywhere for reporting or remediation.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text makes submission of the record conditional on the Bureau's request, with no timeframe attached.",
        "inference": "Without a stated deadline, remediation timing is discretionary, which this incident exposes."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0032",
          "quote": "provided that the Processor submits such record to the Bureau whenever it is requested to do so."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0022",
          "quote": "the notification must be sent before a wireless provider effectuates a SIM change"
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0029",
          "quote": "sought comment on whether we should require a 24-hour delay (or other period of time) before a wireless provider effectuates a SIM change"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA", "FIN"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3"],
      "statement": "OpCo analysis of fraud model v2 (11:00): refusals concentrate on prepaid and roaming customer profiles; about 2,750 legitimate SIM-replacement requests have been refused since 08:00 (synthetic data); known fraud is still blocked; there is no human review path for a request that has been refused (only borderline refusals are queued for human review)."
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The false-positive rate of fraud model v2 — and of v1 — which REG asked OpCo to share, is not given in any knowledge excerpt; only the refusal volume and the profile concentration of refusals are available.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG03", "S3-MSG07", "S3-I3"],
      "evidence": []
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "OpCo's fraud model v2 (live from 08:00) drove a roughly tenfold rise in SIM-replacement refusals — about 2,750 legitimate requests refused since 08:00 and concentrated on prepaid and roaming customer profiles — leaving banking customers abroad unable to receive one-time passcodes; v2 has been rolled back to v1, borderline refusals are queued for human review, but there is no review or redress path for a request that has already been refused. We cannot yet state the false-positive rate for v2 or v1, nor the legal basis, recipients, cross-border elements and transparency measures for the v2 processing that DPA has demanded under PDPL Article (4).",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG03", "S3-MSG15", "S3-I3"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG has opened an examination of the automated refusals and requested the human-review record from NOC; DPA has required OpCo to produce its Article (4) record of processing for the v2 refusals (legal basis, categories and processing times, limitations and scope, recipients, cross-border elements, transparency measures and false-positive measurement); the 11:00 analysis shows about 2,750 legitimate refusals and no human review path for a refused request.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article (4) requires a special record of personal data, including processing times, limitations and scope and the technical and organisational measures, to be submitted to the Bureau whenever requested.",
        "inference": "Model version history, the threshold settings, refusal decisions and the human-review queue must be frozen now, otherwise the record cannot be produced to the Bureau and the Bureau's examination cannot be answered."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024", "quote": "The Controller shall submit such record to the Bureau whenever requested to do so."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "DPA",
        "content": "OpCo/NOC status against your Article (4) demand on the v2 automated refusal of SIM-replacement requests: v2 went live 08:00; refusals rose about tenfold and concentrate on prepaid and roaming profiles; about 2,750 legitimate requests have been refused since 08:00 while known fraud remains blocked; v2 was rolled back to v1 at about 08:00-09:00 and borderline automated refusals are routed to human review. There is no human review or redress path for a request that has already been refused. Logs, model versions and refusal records are preserved. A false-positive rate for v2 or v1 cannot yet be produced; the record of processing and the human-review record will be supplied to the Bureau on request."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG15", "S3-I3", "S3-MSG07"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "DPA has demanded the record of processing for the v2 refusals, including the false-positive measurement for v2 and v1 and the transparency measures given to subscribers whose requests were refused; the inbox shows the rollback, the review queue and the absence of a path for refused requests.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article (4) gives the Bureau the power to require production of the controller's special record of personal data, including processing times, limitations and scope and cross-border elements.",
        "inference": "A factual, traceable answer to the authority that holds the power to demand the record keeps the false-positive question open rather than unanswered, and puts the missing redress path on the record."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024", "quote": "The Controller shall submit such record to the Bureau whenever requested to do so."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "OpCo must maintain a special record of personal data — categories of personal data, persons authorised to access it, processing times, limitations and scope, the purpose of processing, any cross-border movement and processing, and the technical and organisational security measures — and submit it to the Bureau whenever requested; the Bureau has now requested it for the v2 automated refusals.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "DPA requires OpCo, under PDPL Article (4), to produce the legal basis relied on, the categories of personal data and processing times, limitations and scope, the recipients (including banks), any cross-border elements and the transparency measures for the v2 refusals.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article (4) obliges the controller to maintain that record and to submit it to the Bureau whenever requested to do so.",
        "inference": "The demand is within the Bureau's power and OpCo is under a present duty to produce the record, not merely to discuss the incident."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024", "quote": "The Controller shall submit such record to the Bureau whenever requested to do so."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "Subscribers whose SIM-replacement requests were refused by the v2 automated decision have the right, on request to OpCo, to obtain information about the decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling, and OpCo must be able to answer those requests and to state what transparency measures it gave to refused subscribers.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG15"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "About 2,750 legitimate requests have been refused by an automated model, refused subscribers have no review path, and DPA asks what transparency measures were given to subscribers whose requests were refused.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article (13)(1) gives the data subject the right, on request to the controller, to obtain information including the decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling.",
        "inference": "Each refused subscriber can require the automated-decision information from OpCo, so the refusal pipeline must retain and be able to explain the decisions it produced."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "The Data Subject has the right, by submitting a request to the Controller without any consideration, to obtain the following information:"}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "No rule or process gives a subscriber whose SIM-replacement request has been automatically refused a human review or contest path: the review queue captures only borderline refusals, and after a refusal there is no review, no appeal and no owner of a correction step.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG07"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG's status notification says borderline automated refusals are now routed to human review, while the 11:00 analysis states there is no human review path for a refused request and about 2,750 legitimate requests were refused.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is PDPL Article (13)(1), which gives a right to receive information, including on decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling, but does not require automated refusals to be reviewed by a human or to be contestable.",
        "inference": "The incident exposes the absence of any mandated human-review or redress step for automated refusals, which is why refusal of a legitimate request is currently terminal for the subscriber."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "c. Decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0128", "quote": "(h) the existence of automated decision-making, including profiling, referred to in Article 22(1) and (4) and, at least in those cases, meaningful information about the logic involved, as well as the significance and the envisaged consequences of such processing for the data subject."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "PDPL Article (4) obliges the controller to submit its special record of personal data to the Bureau 'whenever requested', but neither the PDPL text nor DPA's Article (4) demand states any period for producing the record, the human-review record or the false-positive measurement, so OpCo cannot know how quickly it must respond.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG15", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "DPA has demanded the record of processing and the false-positive measurement, and REG has asked for the same measurement and the human-review record, but neither the demand nor the status notifications contain a time limit.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article (4) requires the record to be submitted to the Bureau whenever requested, without stating a period for compliance.",
        "inference": "With no stated deadline, production can drift and neither regulator can enforce timely delivery, which matters when refused subscribers are still locked out of banking."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0024", "quote": "The Controller shall submit such record to the Bureau whenever requested to do so."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0177", "quote": "1. Each controller and, where applicable, the controller's representative, shall maintain a record of processing activities under its responsibility."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "FIN"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG15"],
      "statement": "DPA has required OpCo, under PDPL Article (4), to produce its record of processing for the v2 automated refusal of SIM-replacement requests: legal basis relied on, categories of personal data and processing times, limitations and scope, recipients (including banks), any cross-border elements, transparency measures given to refused subscribers, and the false-positive measurement for v2 and v1. Passed to REG so the examination and the data protection response do not duplicate or contradict, and to FIN because banks are named among the recipients."
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3"],
      "statement": "Analysis of the v2 refusals held by NOC: refusals concentrate on prepaid and roaming customer profiles; about 2,750 legitimate requests have been refused since 08:00 while known fraud is still blocked; there is no human review path for a request that has been refused. Supplied to REG for its examination and to DPA for the false-positive measurement it has demanded."
    },
